# Engineering Challenges and System Debugging Notes

## Overview

This phase of the project became heavily focused on identifying, isolating, and correcting real-world inertial estimation problems rather than simply adding new features.

Many of the most important lessons emerged from system instability, unexpected telemetry behavior, and long-duration drift testing.

The project increasingly resembled real embedded systems debugging and estimation analysis rather than straightforward Arduino programming.

---

## Major Challenges Encountered

### 1. False Orientation (“Ghost Tilt”)

One of the largest issues encountered was a persistent false tilt estimate while the sensor remained physically flat.

Symptoms:

* fused orientation estimate slowly drifted upward
* complementary filter appeared unstable
* system falsely believed the IMU was rotating over time

Initial debugging suggested gyro drift alone was responsible, but deeper analysis revealed the problem was layered across multiple mathematical subsystems.

---

### 2. Accelerometer Gravity Reference Corruption

A critical mathematical mistake was identified in the accelerometer preprocessing pipeline.

The system originally removed the full Z-axis gravity component:

```cpp id="n4n6gf"
corrected_az = sample.az - az_offset;
```

This unintentionally destroyed the gravity-vector geometry required for pitch estimation.

Consequences:

* flat orientation produced false 40° pitch estimates
* complementary filter inherited incorrect reference behavior
* fused orientation estimates became unstable

This became one of the most important conceptual lessons of the project:
accelerometer orientation estimation depends on preserving the gravity vector.

---

### 3. Gyroscope Drift Accumulation

Even after initial gyro calibration, long-duration testing revealed that very small residual gyro biases accumulated into large orientation drift over time.

Observed behavior:

* gyro angle continuously climbed while stationary
* tiny bias errors integrated forever
* drift remained measurable over multi-minute tests

This demonstrated a major inertial systems reality:
small sensor errors become large state-estimation errors when integrated over time.

---

### 4. Fixed Timing Assumptions

The original implementation assumed perfectly fixed loop timing:

```cpp id="lj7k8n"
const float dt = 0.10;
```

Real embedded execution timing varied slightly due to:

* serial transmission delays
* loop execution variability
* I2C communication timing

This created integration inaccuracies over long durations.

The solution involved transitioning to real elapsed-time integration:

```cpp id="j6jlwm"
float dt = (now - lastSample) / 1000.0;
```

This improved long-term integration stability and reinforced the importance of timing integrity in real-time systems.

---

### 5. Complementary Filter Tuning

Balancing the complementary filter required understanding the tradeoff between:

* gyro responsiveness
* accelerometer stability

Challenges included:

* excessive gyro trust caused drift accumulation
* excessive accelerometer trust caused vibration sensitivity
* incorrect tuning amplified instability rather than reducing it

This phase demonstrated that sensor fusion tuning is both mathematical and experimental.

---

### 6. Real-World Sensor Noise

The system exhibited multiple forms of real physical noise:

* vibration
* desk impacts
* transient spikes
* accelerometer shock corruption
* electrical instability during early hardware testing

Observations showed:

* accelerometers react strongly to vibration and impacts
* gyroscopes drift even while stationary
* sensor fusion is necessary because neither sensor is reliable alone

---

### 7. Telemetry-Driven Debugging

As complexity increased, debugging by intuition became ineffective.

The project increasingly relied on:

* CSV telemetry logging
* long-duration datasets
* drift-rate analysis
* stationary validation experiments
* comparative signal analysis

This represented a major transition from:
“watching Serial Monitor output”
into:
structured telemetry-driven engineering analysis.

---

## Important Engineering Lessons Learned

### Sensor Fusion Exists Because Sensors Fail Differently

Accelerometers:

* stable long-term
* noisy under motion

Gyroscopes:

* smooth short-term
* drift long-term

The complementary filter works because it balances opposite failure modes.

---

### Small Errors Matter in Real-Time Systems

Tiny residual biases:

* accumulate through integration
* slowly corrupt orientation estimates
* become visible only during long-duration testing

This reinforced the importance of:

* calibration
* validation
* timing accuracy
* telemetry analysis

---

### Debugging Embedded Systems Is Layered

Many failures initially appeared to originate from one subsystem but were actually caused by interactions between:

* timing
* filtering
* geometry
* calibration
* integration
* fusion tuning

The debugging process became highly iterative and multi-disciplinary.

---

